 man exhibits  demanding that Lazarus relieve his  thirst or the disturbing insistence on  the lack of a second chance for the rich  man and for his family doesn't our God  remain merciful into the next life in  our self centered self indulgence do we  assume there must always be yet another  chance to get it right when we've done  precious little to discover what that  might be all along the narrative power  of the story draws us in even as it  turns our comfortable world upside down  the rich person in this story is a stock  character the rich person actually has  no name we've been so disturbed by this  we give him a name we call him D vase  which is just the Latin word for rich  man in the Greek the gender inclusive  anthropos identifies  simply as a rich person and I need to  remind us the rich person can also be a  rich woman dressed in the finest  clothing of the day equivalent perhaps  to Armani salvatore ferragamo or your  own designer of choice he not only dines  well but feasts sumptuously every day  the contrast hits us immediately Lazarus  is a poor person but unlike the masses  of our nameless poor who remain  invisible to us both inside and outside  our gates this man is dignified by a  name he is covered with open source  rather than with fine fabrics he is  hungry and longs for crumbs that fall  from the rich person's table that even  the dogs get to eat even worse the dogs  lick his open source an image that is  pretty repulsive to us to say nothing  about the potential for infection today  we know the rich and famous by name they  enjoy comfort and pleasure while the  Lazarus is suffer extreme hunger  invisibility and neglect when death  comes for both of them however their  fortunes are reversed angels carry  Lazarus to heaven where Abraham welcomes  him with a warm embrace God keeps God's  promise to the poor and the oppressed  the rich person is not so fortunate he  has been cast down while Lazarus has  been raised up not yet able to reflect  on his own behavior or choices in life  he pleads with Abraham to send Lazarus  to relieve his thirst he really hasn't  gotten it yet in the story once the rich  person needs something he suddenly knows  exactly who the poor man is calling him  by name all that he did nothing in his  earthly life to relieve Lazarus is  suffering he appeals to  Abraham to give Lazarus yet another  assignment on behalf of a rich person in  response Abraham explains the reversal  while the rich person enjoyed comfort in  his earthly life Lazarus suffered here  in the afterlife it is only fitting that  their fates are reversed not only that  but there is a condition none of us  quite wants to admit into consciousness  a great chasm separates them what once  was a permeable boundary has become a  permanent barrier that cannot be crossed  from either side it is only when he's  confronted with this unexpected impasse  fixing him in his misery that the rich  person shows any concern for anyone  beyond himself from his still  unconverted point of view Lazarus is a  servant whom Abraham should send to the  rich man's brothers to warm them of  their perilous situation even in  paradise the fortunate poor person's  role is to serve the rich the rich man  hopes that his brothers might listen to  the ghostly Lazarus warning them from  beyond the grave but Abraham refuses and  insists that Moses and the prophets have  already been quite clear about what is  required even though the rich man is in  his chase and conditions thinks his  relatives might listen to one who  returns to them from the dead Abraham is  adamant not only will they not listen to  one return from the dead they will not  even be convinced should one have been  risen from the dead I don't know about  you but that final illusion captures our  attention because it can only apply to  us we do have the experience of one who  has not only returned from the dead  but risen from the dead and we have  great difficulty taking to heart our  responsibilities to those in our  community and Beyond who suffer now  people who are poor oppressed and  suffering continue to remain invisible  to the comfortable rich we are perhaps  tidier about the way we maintain the  chasm between rich and poor than first  century Palestine if we reflect on our  own situation of privilege I am struck  by the invisibility of persons who are  poor as we consign the impoverished to  neighborhoods we never visit the chasm  that confronts the rich person in the  afterlife he first created in this one  we translate I think today chasms into  walls the miles upon miles of concrete  and barbed wire fence fencing we are  busy building along our border with  Mexico to keep our poor neighbors out of  our neighborhoods building walls is more  attractive to us than addressing the  hunger and poverty at the root of this  migration I think of the wall built  between Palestine and Israel confining  the poor less deserving Palestinians to  perpetual need in poverty by depriving  them of their ability to work and farm  beyond their own neighborhoods I think  of African American poverty in New  Orleans still entrenched five years  after Katrina and pretty much out of  sight again and I think about some of  our neighborhoods in New Haven at st.  Thomas More Chapel one morning just as  school was starting a student's mom told  me she was advised not to drive down  dixwell avenue that it wasn't safe on a  sunday morning really most of the folks  I had just seen on dixwell on my way to  church were all themselves going to  their own churches not say  but I could also not help but notice  that some of the neighborhoods along the  way are poor we too easily seem to  develop habits of avoiding the reminders  that we do not all enjoy the same  standard of living I also think of the  Pakistanis devastated by floods their  homes gone their farmlands ruined and  our rather anemic response along with  other first world countries to help them  some of you may have participated last  night in the benefit here trying to  mobilize a stronger response within this  community sometimes our poor and  suffering brothers and sisters are only  too clearly in view through the internet  and other media and this can become so  overwhelming we suffer compassion  fatigue if we care and we have an excuse  for remaining indifferent if we don't in  addition to the chasms that separate  rich and poor I was also drawn to think  about the crumbs from the rich person's  table for which Lazarus longs our world  is more complicated than first century  Palestine we know much more about the  causes of hunger than those in the world  of the parable I recently read that the  food we waste could actually feed the  world a stunning amount of the food we  grow never gets from the farm to our  plates food is wasted at every step  along the way of our own food chain a  2004 study estimates that our food waste  might be close to fifty percent an  astounding fifty million tons of food  wasted each year we desperately need to  find efficient ways to get this food to  those who need it and can benefit from  it related to these wasted crumbs from  the tables of first world countries is  the distribution of land and food  resources that favor the rich the  insistence on exporting food from all  over the world to us has resulted in  causing these countries to export the  food they grow to us while their own  people go hungry creative agricultural  movements that advocate we begin to grow  our own food again might stop us from  impoverishing our poor neighbors so we  can have every food in season or out of  season but we will be unable to mobilize  sufficient reform until we really  understand the largely invisible and  impersonal processes around food  resources and politics that maintain  unnecessary levels of poverty both here  and abroad finally I think of the poor  in our midst whom we either ignore or do  not see maybe even closer to home among  those of us who inhabit the rarefied  world of Ivy League education we made to  easily be tempted to assume that we are  all equally privileged certainly all of  us who work or teach or study or  research at Yale are privileged we are  privileged wealthy in our intellectual  gifts and in terms of the educational  background we bring to this community  but we do not all equally share the same  sense of entitlement about how we are  treated and treat others because of the  generous levels of financial aid there  are enormous class and cultural  differences among us that often remain  quite invisible who are the Lazarus's  among us who among us here in this  Chapel this morning despite our  privilege feel more like Lazarus than  the rich person who among us is anxious  about how well we're doing academically  who among us is feeling alone and  displaced without  support of our families and previous  circle of friends and community who  among us longs for the crumbs of  acknowledgement of being seen and  responded to simply because each one of  us is God's beloved and we are called to  create a rich and supportive community  for one another who among us is  struggling to look right to fit in to be  accepted whether or not we can afford it  how many among us not only are studying  but also working long hours to manage  our expenses are we willing to cross  this invisible chasm among us now or do  we carefully ignore the subtle signs of  such suffering as we allow the gospel to  question our assumptions our sense of  entitlement and our unresponsiveness to  the Lazarus's in our midst does not the  gospel also reassure us that today we  have the possibility of responding to  those who suffer in our midst today we  still have the possibility of bridging  one of the chasms that separates us  today we can share our food with one  another even as we work toward long-term  solutions today we can visit another  neighborhood today we can recommit  ourselves to the mercy and compassion to  which Jesus calls us because we live  today God meets us here both comforting  us and inviting us to open our hearts to  one another today we can open our hearts  both to the Lazarus's in our midst and  outside our walls and to the privileged  among us who have yet to heat the law in  the prophets today we can open ourselves  to the full demands of discipleship to  which Jesus calls us today we can live  in  a radical gratitude for your boundless  generosity God and we address you in the  words of walter brueggemann you give  your word to call the world into being  you give your sovereign rule to  emancipate the slaves and they oppressed  you give your commanding fidelity to  form your own people you give your life  for the life of the world broken bread  that feeds pour it out wine that binds  and heals you give we receive and we are  thankful
 

